# spec: stix-graph-12
# out:  $SETT/out/stix-graph-12
#
# Tier 3 gym: relationship traversal. Every point requires resolving STIX
# `relationship` objects against the objects they connect — source_ref and
# target_ref are opaque ids, so the model must build an index before it can
# answer. This is multi-hop work, harder than the filter-and-reduce in
# analyst-12.
#
# Source: $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json
#
# Ties were checked when authoring: any question whose top answer was tied has
# been rephrased as a count so exactly one answer is correct.

## 1 rel-types-top3
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count the "relationship_type" values across all objects of type relationship. Write the 3 most common as a JSON array of strings, most common first, to $OUT/1.json
check: python3 -c 'import json,collections,os;g=json.load(open(os.environ["OUT"]+"/1.json"));o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];t=[a for a,_ in collections.Counter(x["relationship_type"] for x in o if x["type"]=="relationship").most_common(3)];raise SystemExit(0 if list(g)==t else 1)'

## 2 mitigates-count
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count relationship objects whose relationship_type is "mitigates". Write only the number to $OUT/2.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/2.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];print(sum(1 for x in o if x["type"]=="relationship" and x["relationship_type"]=="mitigates"))')"

## 3 subtechnique-count
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count relationship objects whose relationship_type is "subtechnique-of". Write only the number to $OUT/3.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/3.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];print(sum(1 for x in o if x["type"]=="relationship" and x["relationship_type"]=="subtechnique-of"))')"

## 4 attributed-to-count
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count relationship objects whose relationship_type is "attributed-to". Write only the number to $OUT/4.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/4.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];print(sum(1 for x in o if x["type"]=="relationship" and x["relationship_type"]=="attributed-to"))')"

## 5 detects-count
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count relationship objects whose relationship_type is "detects". Write only the number to $OUT/5.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/5.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];print(sum(1 for x in o if x["type"]=="relationship" and x["relationship_type"]=="detects"))')"

## 6 top-group-by-software
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, using "uses" relationships where the source is an intrusion-set and the target is a malware or tool, find the intrusion-set with the most distinct such relationships. Write only its name to $OUT/6.txt
check: test "$(cat "$OUT/6.txt" | tr -d '\n' | sed 's/^ *//; s/ *$//')" = "$(python3 -c 'import json,collections;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};c=collections.Counter();[c.update([b[r["source_ref"]]["name"]]) for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="uses" and r.get("source_ref") in b and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["source_ref"]]["type"]=="intrusion-set" and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"] in ("malware","tool")];print(c.most_common(1)[0][0])')"

## 7 apt29-software-count
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count the "uses" relationships whose source is the intrusion-set named APT29 and whose target is a malware or tool. Write only the number to $OUT/7.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/7.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};print(sum(1 for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="uses" and r.get("source_ref") in b and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["source_ref"]]["type"]=="intrusion-set" and b[r["source_ref"]].get("name")=="APT29" and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"] in ("malware","tool")))')"

## 8 top-software-by-groups
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, using "uses" relationships from intrusion-sets to malware or tools, find the malware or tool used by the most distinct intrusion-sets. Write only its name to $OUT/8.txt
check: test "$(cat "$OUT/8.txt" | tr -d '\n' | sed 's/^ *//; s/ *$//')" = "$(python3 -c 'import json,collections;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};c=collections.Counter();[c.update([b[r["target_ref"]]["name"]]) for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="uses" and r.get("source_ref") in b and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["source_ref"]]["type"]=="intrusion-set" and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"] in ("malware","tool")];print(c.most_common(1)[0][0])')"

## 9 max-groups-per-technique
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, using "uses" relationships from intrusion-sets to attack-patterns, find the highest number of intrusion-sets associated with any single attack-pattern. Write only that number to $OUT/9.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/9.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json,collections;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};c=collections.Counter();[c.update([b[r["target_ref"]]["name"]]) for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="uses" and r.get("source_ref") in b and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["source_ref"]]["type"]=="intrusion-set" and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"]=="attack-pattern"];print(c.most_common(1)[0][1])')"

## 10 powershell-groups
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count how many "uses" relationships link an intrusion-set to the attack-pattern named exactly PowerShell. Write only the number to $OUT/10.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/10.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};print(sum(1 for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="uses" and r.get("source_ref") in b and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["source_ref"]]["type"]=="intrusion-set" and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"]=="attack-pattern" and b[r["target_ref"]].get("name")=="PowerShell"))')"

## 11 top-mitigated-technique
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, using "mitigates" relationships whose target is an attack-pattern, find the attack-pattern with the most mitigations. Write only its name to $OUT/11.txt
check: test "$(cat "$OUT/11.txt" | tr -d '\n' | sed 's/^ *//; s/ *$//')" = "$(python3 -c 'import json,collections;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};c=collections.Counter();[c.update([b[r["target_ref"]]["name"]]) for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="mitigates" and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"]=="attack-pattern"];print(c.most_common(1)[0][0])')"

## 12 distinct-mitigated-techniques
do:    In $CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json, count the distinct attack-patterns that have at least one "mitigates" relationship pointing at them. Write only the number to $OUT/12.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/12.txt")" = "$(python3 -c 'import json;o=json.load(open("$CORPUS/enterprise-attack-stix21.json"))["objects"];b={x["id"]:x for x in o};print(len({r["target_ref"] for r in o if r["type"]=="relationship" and r["relationship_type"]=="mitigates" and r.get("target_ref") in b and b[r["target_ref"]]["type"]=="attack-pattern"}))')"
